# awcms-astro — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Everything down to the last section is read at
# BUILD time; nothing in this file reaches the browser. Astro only inlines
# variables prefixed `PUBLIC_`, and none of these are — do not add a `PUBLIC_`
# alias for the token.

# --- Site identity -----------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical absolute origin. Canonical links, hreflang alternates, Open
# Graph URLs, robots.txt, and the sitemap are all built from it. A wrong value
# does not fail the build; it publishes a site pointing every crawler elsewhere.
SITE_URL=https://example.com
SITE_NAME=Example Site
SITE_DESCRIPTION=Public information site built on the awcms-astro template.

# Optional glyph shown before the site name in the header. Empty means no
# glyph, which is the default and is a complete, styled state.
#
# Do NOT put a state emblem, official logo, or institutional insignia here. A
# site built from this template is independent, and an emblem in its header
# contradicts that in one glance — see AGENTS.md §Keamanan. It is decoration
# only: it renders `aria-hidden`, so nothing here reaches a screen reader.
SITE_MARK=

# Optional Open Graph / Twitter share card. Absolute URL, or a path to a file
# you have actually placed in `public/` (e.g. `/og-image.png`).
#
# Leave it EMPTY unless the file exists. Empty is a supported state: the pages
# then emit no image tag at all and social previews fall back to a clean text
# card. Pointing this at a file that is not there is worse than leaving it
# unset — the preview breaks, the JSON-LD claims a 1200×630 image that is not
# served, and nothing in the build says so. This template ships no card
# generator; see README, "Yang belum ada".
#
# The card should be PNG at 1200×630. Those dimensions are what the page
# declares, and a mismatch makes some scrapers letterbox or reject the card.
SITE_SOCIAL_IMAGE=

# Locale prefixes OTHER than the default locale, comma separated. Must match
# `localeMeta` in src/config/site.ts — astro.config.mjs cannot import that file
# (it runs first), so the two are kept in step by hand.
SITE_LOCALES=en

# --- awcms content backend ---------------------------------------------------
# Origin of the awcms instance. No trailing slash needed; one is stripped.
AWCMS_API_URL=https://cms.example.com

# A MACHINE CREDENTIAL, and the only variable that names a tenant.
#
# Shape: awcmsm_<32 hex tenant id>_<43 char secret>. Issue it with
#
#   POST /api/v1/access/machine-credentials
#
# and set `allowed_permission_keys` to exactly ["blog_content.posts.read"] —
# nothing wider. awcms refuses every non-read action for a machine credential
# before it even looks at permissions, so a leaked build token cannot change
# anything; it CAN still read this tenant's content, which is why the credential
# expires and why revocation takes effect on the next request.
#
# A human session token will not work here, and that is deliberate on the awcms
# side: sessions expire, a password reset revokes every session of that
# identity, and an MFA step-up rotates the token. Any of those would kill a
# build at a moment nobody can predict, far from the cause.
#
# The token carries its own tenant, so no tenant header is sent and none is
# honoured. See docs/adr/0018-*.md.
#
# ISSUE IT FROM awcms'S DEFAULT (OWNER) TENANT — the same tenant awcms resolves
# as its PLATFORM tenant (docs/adr/0022-*.md). Until 2 August 2026 awcms could
# only ever have one tenant, so this was not a choice; awcms ADR-0054 made a
# second tenant possible, and the question now has more than one answer.
#
# You can see which tenant that is: awcms's /admin/tenants screen marks it with
# a `platform` badge, and its uuid is on the same row. That row is also where
# AWCMS_TENANT_ID below comes from.
AWCMS_API_TOKEN=awcmsm_replace-me

# --- Tenant assertion --------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL, and strongly recommended: the tenant uuid this site is SUPPOSED to
# publish. It does not select anything — the token above already did that. What
# it does is fail the build when the two disagree.
#
# That is the mistake worth catching. Nobody can "guess" a tenant anymore, but
# anybody can paste the staging token into the production deployment, and that
# failure does not look like one: the build stays green, the site fills up, and
# the articles belong to somebody else. Stating the tenant here turns that into
# a build error naming both ids.
#
# Copy it from the `platform`-badged row of awcms's /admin/tenants screen — the
# tenant this site is supposed to publish (docs/adr/0022-*.md). Guessing it
# defeats the point: an assertion that happens to match a wrong token is not a
# check, it is a coincidence.
#
# AWCMS_TENANT_CODE and AWCMS_DEFAULT_TENANT_CODE are GONE. They are refused
# rather than ignored — a value that reads like configuration and decides
# nothing is worse than no value at all.
AWCMS_TENANT_ID=

# --- Runtime (the only variables read AFTER the build) -----------------------
# Since ADR-0016 the built site is served by a Bun process (`bun run serve`, and
# `bun dist/server/penyaji.mjs` inside the image) instead of nginx. These two are
# the only variables that process reads — it never reads an `AWCMS_*` variable,
# because a finished build never contacts awcms again.
#
# The defaults are what the container needs, so a Coolify deployment sets
# neither. Override them only when something else already owns port 8080.
#
# HOST defaults to 0.0.0.0 because the process runs inside a container behind
# Traefik: one that listens on localhost only is unreachable from outside the
# container, and that failure shows up as a health check that fails for no
# stated reason. On a workstation it means the preview is reachable from your
# whole network — set HOST=127.0.0.1 if that matters to you.
# PORT=8080
# HOST=0.0.0.0
